00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it was a doomsday scenario on our  doorsteps  lockdowns cities deserted  travel disrupted the bonds that bind us  for millions it meant working from home  or else remote learning online exercise  it's been almost 18 months we know the  death toll we know the damage it did to  our economies  but what did being cut off from each  other due to our  mental well-being  welcome to our kobe 19 special i'm  monica jones good to have you with us  so how are you today coping with  all the pandemic restrictions well i  have to admit that  after the past 18 months i'm slightly  more on edge these days  and despite lower infection rates i'm  still hesitant about  meeting other people i wonder where this  will end  but if the pandemic can affect me what  must it be like for people  already struggling with anxieties  [Music]  out of his head and into his body  martin's drumsticks  are his anchor when everything around  him seems to fall apart  the 33 year old has suffered from  depression for years  but his illness has been at its worst  during the pandemic  everything crumbled his therapy the  structure of his daily life  his whole stability chapman  went to the park and tried to shut it  all out  it was just too much for me i didn't  want to see  or hear anything it feels like you're  getting sucked  into absolute emptiness and that doesn't  feel very nice  veronica finds peace in literature  reading poetry outside  she has also suffered from depression  for years but during the pandemic  the 23 year old became so depressed that  she needed hospital treatment  sometimes it was so bad that she could  no longer find any meaning in life  it's become analysis  my thoughts start going round in circles  i think about suicide  i have the urge to hurt myself that's  been part of my life for years  and i have very little motivation even  if i wanted to do something  i couldn't bring myself to do it  people suffering from depression are hit  particularly hard by the fears and  restrictions of the pandemic  also because therapies were often broken  off during lockdown  but the curbs on freedom also affect  people not suffering from clinical  depression  but experts do not see the pandemic in  itself  as a trigger for depression  people have definitely experienced  severe mood swings  but that doesn't mean that we will see a  new wave of patience with depression  the illness is quite separate from  external factors  that means if you don't have the  predisposition  you don't slip into it even if life has  been hard on you  veronica speaks openly on social media  about what she has gone through during  lockdown  about her suffering but also about her  successes  it has been a learning experience  i've learned to stand up for myself when  i have problems  if i feel uncomfortable i say so so that  something can be done about it  i've also learned to be more comfortable  on my own  it doesn't work for long but it's okay  for a few days  right now neither veronica nor martin  feel like being alone  martin's looking forward to getting back  together with his band soon  well for more on this i'm joined by  elise paul she's a senior research  fellow  in the research department of behavioral  science and health at university college  in london good to have you with us  let's just start straight away with what  we heard in this report uh  where it just showed that uh some  patients some people  that suffer from depression they've  turned to social media as a tool to  self-medicate when therapy  was not available do you think that  that's a good alternative  it certainly depends on what people are  using  social media for um if they are using it  to  disclose you know difficulties and seek  support that could be a positive thing  but if they're using it to sort of avoid  you know coping with their difficulties  then that could be a negative thing  we've also found that  excessive looking for new scrolling for  news on kovid  was related to declines in mental health  so it really does depend  how you're using social media  how well are therapies actually prepared  for dealing with the the additional  problems that their patients are  experiencing now because of the pandemic  well i think it's difficult to say as an  epidemiologist i can't speak on behalf  of therapists but i will  i would say that you know as therapists  are people too  even though we've all been impacted in  different ways we have all gone through  the pandemic together over the past year  and a half  um and there will be an increased need  for therapy  i hope that this will be an opportunity  for  governments policymakers um and even in  the general population  to pay more attention to mental health  and to  direct more resources in that direction  of course we've also heard in this  report that  while a lot of people obviously feeling  the weight of the pandemic i just  mentioned something myself there as well  that  cannot be compared to a real serious  clinical  depression so people sort of being moody  now  is that just a face or is there perhaps  the danger that they'll actually fall  into a proper depression  well i mean i think that it's important  to consider how  those mood swings and how the  irritability is affecting one's life  um if it is making it really difficult  to carry out your activities  things that you need to do if it's  impacting your work  your parenting your relationships then  it is important to seek help  you know whether or not you feel you  meet some clinical threshold i think  it's important to consider  how it is impacting one's life i mean  how do you  how do you notice how can you  differentiate also as an outsider  perhaps to see okay this person now is  really in danger of not just being  not just having a day off but actually  being being ill  well excessive days off from work are  certainly  uh one sign um another that friends and  family could notice  is avoidance behaviors so some people  tend to withdraw  and have some of the classic symptoms of  depression that we typically think of  such as sleeping too much  you know reduced energy slow moving  other people can have things like  irritability  you know lashing out a lot of anger so i  think it can  it is important to acknowledge that it  can look different  in different people you mentioned uh  something else about these of um  awareness of mental  mental illness is is has risen now  because of that  that could actually be a good thing an  upside to the pandemic or am i reading  too much into that  no i think it's definitely a good thing  i hope that will come out of this  pandemic  i think that you know it's just  unprecedented that we've been cut off  from things that we enjoy art you know  we've gotten the opportunity to think  about what is the difference between  attending a concert live versus seeing  it online  what's it like to be in a room with five  of your friends versus  on a walk on a rainy day with one of  your friends and so we've really  had the opportunity to think about and  as researchers too and to study  what is it about our daily lives that is  necessary  to maintain mental health um you know  whether or not we've slipped into a  depression but just on a daily basis  all right and last question a personal  one how do you  cope with the pressure of the pandemic  yeah so i try to do things every day  that just keep my body and mind  able to cope the best i can i drink lots  of water  i try to go to bed every night at the  same time wake up at the same time  i eat healthy i watch my caffeine and  alcohol intake  i exercise every day and i also meditate  every day i have meditation apps on my  phone  even if it's just five minutes in the  morning it really helps me to  internalize  a calm meaning right good good advice  thank you so much elise poor senior  research fellow and the research  department of behavioral science and  health  at university college in london  time for your questions now and over to  derek  [Music]  how can genetic engineering be helpful  in fighting upcoming pandemics  genetic engineering is one of those  phrases that get thrown around a lot but  i found a  a surprising number of people don't  actually know what it means so so let's  define it  before moving forward when you  genetically engineer something you use  tools from fields that are usually now  grouped under the label  biotechnology to manipulate  and modify an organism's genome  the goal is to make it do things the  organism  wouldn't do otherwise for example  produce  a highly specific medicinal product or  produce it  in faster ways or produce it in larger  amounts  genetic engineering methods are now  common in areas  from from crop science to waste  mitigation  to sustainable fuel development but when  it comes to modern  medicine genetically modified  microorganisms play a really  central role nowadays for instance  microbes have been engineered to churn  out  much of the insulin that people with  diabetes  need to survive and and gene editing  methods are also key  to designing new medications they're  used not only  to manufacture a complex biologics like  like monoclonal antibodies but but also  vaccines  viral vector vaccines like those made by  astrazeneca  are like like poster child examples of  products made  with genetic engineering they work  because the designers were able to  splice  genes from stars cov2 into a harmless  adenovirus that that when injected  can help make you immune to covet 19. so  genetic engineering is much more than  helpful in the fight against pandemics  it'll continue to be a  crucial facet of reacting to them  and containing them in the future  thanks for watching

